There is a real asymmetry there, but I’d phrase it more precisely than “purity.”

For most of human history, maternity was directly observable in a way paternity was not: pregnancy and birth usually make genetic motherhood highly certain, whereas fatherhood had to be inferred from sexual exclusivity, timing, resemblance, social norms, and trust. Before genetic testing, that created a genuine information asymmetry.

That asymmetry plausibly helped shape some recurring male concerns around sexual fidelity, mate guarding, inheritance, lineage, reputation, and certainty of paternity. In a simplified evolutionary frame:

male investment in offspring
→ benefit depends partly on relatedness
→ paternity uncertainty creates risk
→ selection may favor sensitivity to cues of infidelity / resemblance / exclusivity

But that should not be stretched into “men suffer because women don’t have to worry.” Women historically faced a different but equally consequential uncertainty landscape: whether a partner would remain, invest resources, protect offspring, redirect effort to another mate, become coercive, or die. Their reproductive costs from pregnancy and childcare were also much higher.

So the sexes inherited different uncertainty profiles, not one sex getting certainty and the other getting misery.

And “purity” is a cultural overlay on top of that biology. The biological variable is closer to:

confidence of genetic parentage

Cultures then wrapped that in honor, virginity norms, inheritance law, marriage, religion, shame, adultery rules, and property systems.

Your continuity point is interesting, though. A woman historically had a stronger direct signal that this child came through her body, while a man often had to rely more heavily on social continuity:

trust
marriage
resemblance
community recognition
lineage
ritual
law

So some of what looks like obsession with bloodlines may partly be humanity building external institutions to stabilize an internal uncertainty.

Modern DNA testing changes that asymmetry technologically. It turns what used to be probabilistic into something that can be measured directly.

That itself is a nice example of the pattern we’ve been talking about:

when biology leaves an uncertainty, culture builds a story around it; when technology makes the hidden variable measurable, the story can change.